592186278406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176065 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176065 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176065 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176065 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176065 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176065 #edb/ref 1039 "Changing to Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176065 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176065 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1045 "Areas of Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176142 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6a297d5e-701c-5ce4-ab89-16c2883ce6f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176211 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176211 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176211 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176211 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z=xy$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $2\\le y\\le 3.$ What is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176211 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "944c1671-bbab-513c-b727-a199c9a9429b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176211 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=xy,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(y)^{2}+(x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176214 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176214 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176214 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176214 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176214 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40e44b1f-1930-5622-81b2-291ea09677cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176214 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/be5dc3bf3ed4b6b7bee06d583b69e40a3922edede1e61a6f0b68d2ce0767f92a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176242 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2x+3y$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d9b2c67-e61f-5dae-a147-dc5eaf3565f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176242 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2x+3y,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4+9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176243 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the portion of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c85ae37-614c-52e2-a347-0d9aae387c55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176243 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b6de7829e702377d1b20bb1b297d3d28e6fb8bae9672b0137d92b4d53a96c83.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176719 #edb/ref 1061 33399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02eab857-ba70-5ffc-a6e2-2e77e9070917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176720 #edb/ref 1061 33402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dafe3e1a-3347-5546-a43c-a510dbc307fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176721 #edb/ref 1061 33440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40bd7598-b352-53c3-8ac4-1cc184b87de9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176722 #edb/ref 1061 33441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20aafc3a-c734-59a5-9792-c59b83342b12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176747 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176747 #edb/ref 1061 34075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176747 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd0b86de-1ce8-5231-a10e-a2cb302f264c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176747 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176884 #edb/ref 1061 44417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95a582e7-cf2f-5cb4-a271-ae31cf32d50a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176887 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176887 #edb/ref 1061 44744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a2e11edd-ffa0-5587-81ee-94d96ca815b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176989 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nNow, if our surface $S$ is defined as $z= f(x,y),$ then it can always be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x,y)= \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: f(x,y)\\right\\rangle  \\qquad (x,y)  \\in D.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variables $x$ and $y$ act as parameters. \n\nThus, the fundamental vector product of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{∂f}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂f}{∂y},\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand using the fact that $z = f(x,y),$ we can write this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨-\\frac{∂z}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂z}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| = \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that for a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to apply this formula." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176989 #edb/ref 1065 5334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176989 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8319b2f4-f301-522e-944a-a56acf314d83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176989 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176990 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/86/867722106ffd8445b62a98ab43197ac2fba646faff95017ef7e92c254a97fb6e.png)\n\n  \n\nThe surface $S$ shown above consists of the part of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ that lies under the plane $z=9.$ Let's compute the surface area of $S.$\n\nThe plane intersects the paraboloid when $z=9.$ Thus, the curve that describes the intersection points is\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 9,\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nTherefore, our surface can be described by the equations\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2, \\quad (x,y) \\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): \\, x^2 + y^2 \\le 9\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the surface area of $S,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our surface area formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by changing the variables to polar coordinates $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta),$ we have $\\textrm dA = r\\,\\textrm d r\\, \\textrm d \\theta.$ Thus, our integration region becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta): \\, 0\\leq r\\leq 3,\\:0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+4(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,d\\theta \\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{1}{12}{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(37\\sqrt{37}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the area of our surface is \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}(37\\sqrt{37} -1).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176990 #edb/ref 1065 5344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176990 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f3795a6-1971-55b3-9109-8565cd3830a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176990 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177075 #edb/ref 1064 "We have similar formulas for the areas of surfaces defined by $x = f(y,z)$ and $y = f(x,z){:}$\n\n- For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\n- For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177075 #edb/ref 1065 7715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177075 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c7f14be-f96a-5dc7-b3c9-10c1aa305b1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177075 #edb/ref 1067 "Cases Where the Domain Is the XZ- or YZ-Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238162 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238162 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238162 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b7bc11c-66f0-516c-be38-a48045d964f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238601 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238601 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238601 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e74bf5b-a988-54bf-b10b-2eefe28915c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238746 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238746 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759